ac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-1)}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-1)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250127 13194139533771 true] [17592186250126 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250128 13194139533771 true] [17592186250126 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533771 true] [17592186250126 1041 "field-1" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250126 1104 #edb/ref 17592186250127 13194139533771 true] [17592186250126 1110 #uuid "af18e130-03b9-588e-ae2c-61e2f95a436b" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250127 1034 "-\\sin(2x-1)" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533771 true] [17592186250127 1111 #uuid "5394d2d1-2862-54c9-bf13-ac330c8057ac" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250128 1034 "\\sin(2x-1)" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533771 true] [17592186250128 1111 #uuid "9cf509ac-948c-502e-925a-522182506b08" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250129 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250130 13194139533771 true] [17592186250129 1089 "q-343332" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250129 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533771 true] [17592186250129 1100 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{x}$" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250129 1113 #uuid "9a7c912f-983c-5247-a010-73ac3f66fd43" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250129 1145 "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x)+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x)({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250130 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250131 13194139533771 true] [17592186250130 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533771 true] [17592186250130 1041 "field-1" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250130 1104 #edb/ref 17592186250131 13194139533771 true] [17592186250130 1110 #uuid "efc23796-5445-50a2-9807-f38542b4f6f2" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250131 1034 "\\sin(4x)+4\\cos(4x)" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250131 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533771 true] [17592186250131 1111 #uuid "dd2c923a-3229-5cec-8fc8-f0110187612c" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250132 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250133 13194139533771 true] [17592186250132 1089 "q-343546" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250132 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533771 true] [17592186250132 1100 "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the curve $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{x}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250132 1113 #uuid "cfa96c3e-3660-5ea3-8618-6409b518e6bc" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250132 1145 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ and the denominator $v(x)=x.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(x)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(1)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})sin\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-cos\\,(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}{{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(-1)-0}{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250133 1000 #edb/ref 17592186250134 13194139533771 true] [17592186250133 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533771 true] [17592186250133 1041 "field-1" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250133 1104 #edb/ref 17592186250134 13194139533771 true] [17592186250133 1110 #uuid "69397bbd-19b2-59fc-8ad3-025cbb5c2afe" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250134 1034 "\\frac{6}{\\pi}" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533771 true] [17592186250134 1111 #uuid "dcf55986-23c1-53f3-baf2-54dc3e95910e" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250135 1017 #edb/ref 17592186250136 13194139533771 true] [17592186250135 1089 "q-343576" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250135 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533771 true] [17592186250135 1100 "If $y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{x+2},$ find ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n${y}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250135 1113 #uuid "dc7457da-add5-5033-834e-3210499e9469" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250135 1145 "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={e}^{3x}$ and the denominator $v(x)=x+2.$\n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=3{e}^{3x},\\,{v}^{′}(x)=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{e}^{3x})(x+2)-{e}^{3x}(1)}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+6-1)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3x+5)\\,{e}^{3x}}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of ${y}^{′}(x)$ at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{(3(1)+5)({e}^{3(1)})}{((1)+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{e}^{3}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533771 true] [17592186250136 1000 #edb/ref 175